      "text/plain": [
       "'The town of Oakhaven smelled constantly of damp pine and woodsmoke, but inside the shop of Master Silas, it smelled of copper, sweet oil, and old paper. \\n\\nSilas was a clockmaker of rare talent. People said he could make a pendulum swing to the heartbeat of a sleeping robin. But Silas possessed one clock that he had never built, and certainly had never sold.\\n\\nIt was a silver pocket watch, smooth as a river pebble, with a single, unblemished ruby set into the winding crown. It did not tick. It had no face, no hands, and no gears inside. Yet, Silas kept it in his vest pocket, resting against his ribs like a second heart.\\n\\nThe watch was a family heirloom with a dangerous secret: if you pressed the ruby crown, time stopped. \\n\\nRaindrops would freeze in mid-air like glass beads. Flocks of birds would hang suspended above the rooftops. The world would turn quiet, grayscale, and perfectly still, allowing Silas to walk through the frozen moment for as long as he liked. \\n\\nThere was, however, a cost. \\n\\nMagic, Silas’s grandfather had warned him, never works for free. For every minute Silas spent in stolen time, he paid with a memory. Not a random piece of trivia, but a genuine, living piece of his past. A birthday, the face of an old friend, the scent of his mother’s kitchen—stolen time devoured them all, leaving quiet, hollow spaces in his mind.\\n\\nBecause of this, Silas had only used the watch three times in his seventy years. Once to dodge a falling oak branch in his youth, once to catch a slipping apprentice from a high scaffold, and once... well, he couldn\\'t remember the third time. The watch had already taken that memory.\\n\\nOn a rainy Tuesday evening, Silas was polishing the brass gears of a grandfather clock when he heard a sudden, sharp screech outside his shop window. \\n\\nHe looked up. A heavy, iron-wheeled milk wagon was sliding out of control down the steep, rain-slicked cobblestone street. The draft horses were panicked, eyes wide and rolling. And directly in the wagon\\'s path was a little girl in a yellow slicker, crouching to rescue a stray kitten trapped in the gutter.\\n\\nThe driver was pulling frantically on the reins, but the heavy wagon had too much momentum. The girl didn\\'t see it coming.\\n\\nSilas didn\\'t think. His hand shot to his vest pocket. He pulled out the silver pebble and slammed his thumb down on the ruby crown.\\n\\n*Click.*\\n\\nInstantly, the roar of the rain snapped into dead silence. A splash of water raised by the wagon\\'s wheel froze into a delicate sculpture of droplets. The panicked horses stood motionless, muscle and vein carved in stone. The little girl remained crouched, a smile frozen on her face, reaching for the kitten.\\n\\nSilas exhaled a long breath that blossomed into a still cloud of mist. His old knees protested, but he stepped out of his shop and walked into the suspended world. \\n\\nHe reached the girl, lifted her gently by her arms, and carried her to the safety of a dry doorway five feet away. He went back, scooped up the shivering kitten, and placed it safely in her arms. Finally, he stepped out of the street and leaned against his shop\\'s doorframe.\\n\\nHe pressed the ruby crown again.\\n\\n*Click.*\\n\\nThe world exploded back to life with a deafening crash. The milk wagon slammed into a wooden hitching post, shattering the crate on the back, but rolling harmlessly past the spot where the girl had been. The rain pelted down. The girl blinked, suddenly standing in a doorway, looking down at the kitten in her arms with utter confusion, entirely unharmed.\\n\\nSilas slumped against his doorway, gasping for air. \\n\\nThen, the toll was collected.\\n\\nDeep inside his chest, Silas felt a cold, pulling sensation, like a thread being unraveled from a hand-knit sweater. He clutched his head as a warm, bright memory began to dissolve. \\n\\nHe tried to hold onto it, but it slipped through his fingers. He forgot the name of the town he lived in for a terrifying ten seconds. Then that came back, but something else vanished—a vivid, cherished memory of a woman with laugh lines around her eyes, standing in a garden of sunflowers. He remembered loving her deeply, but he could no longer remember her name. He couldn\\'t remember the sound of her voice. She became a silhouette in his own mind.\\n\\nSilas stumbled back into his warm, ticking shop and sat heavily in his armchair. He pulled the silver watch from his pocket. It felt cold.\\n\\nHe realized then why his shop was filled with hundreds of clocks, ticking in unison, marking every second with obsessive precision. He hadn\\'t built them because he loved time. He had built them because he was terrified of losing it. He had spent his life trying to measure the very thing he kept sacrificing.\\n\\nSilas looked down at the watch, then at his hands—calloused, spotted with age, but gentle enough to save a life. He realized he didn\\'t regret saving the girl. If he had to lose a memory to save a life, it was a price worth paying. But he also realized he couldn\\'t keep living in fear of what he might forget next.\\n\\nSlowly, Silas opened the bottom drawer of his workbench. He pulled out a heavy iron strongbox, placed the silver watch inside, locked it with a small key, and tucked the key deep into his pocket.\\n\\nThere was a knock at his shop door. Silas opened it to find the little girl in the yellow slicker, holding the kitten, flanked by her weeping mother, who was pointing at Silas and talking about a miracle.\\n\\n\"Did you see what happened, sir?\" the mother asked, wiping rain from her face. \"It was like she vanished from the street in a blink!\"\\n\\nSilas looked down at the girl, then at the kitten purring against her yellow raincoat. He smiled, his eyes crinkling at the corners.\\n\\n\"I didn\\'t see a thing, ma\\'am,\" Silas said softly, stepping aside to let them into the warmth. \"But come in out of the cold. I\\'ll put the kettle on.\"\\n\\nHe had lost a piece of his yesterday, but as he poured three cups of tea, Master Silas knew he was fully, beautifully present in today.'"
